WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:06.044
A few months ago,

00:00:06.044 --> 00:00:09.172
I surrendered the password
to my Twitter account

00:00:09.172 --> 00:00:13.426
to let a person with paralysis
tweet out their thoughts.

00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:16.137
But I mean that literally.

00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:19.224
Philip O'Keefe can't use
his fingers to type like you or I,

00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:21.977
but thanks to a tiny brain implant,

00:00:21.977 --> 00:00:24.729
he was able to send the following tweets.

00:00:26.231 --> 00:00:29.693
"Hello world! Short tweet.
Monumental progress."

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:33.446
"No need for keystrokes or voices.

00:00:33.488 --> 00:00:36.032
I created this tweet just by thinking it."

00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:38.994
"My hope is that I pave the way

00:00:39.035 --> 00:00:41.997
for people to be able to tweet
through thoughts. Phil."

00:00:44.499 --> 00:00:47.210
Now you might be thinking
there are some people out there

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.088
who should not be allowed
to tweet directly from their brain.

00:00:50.088 --> 00:00:51.423
(Laughter)

00:00:51.423 --> 00:00:52.591
I agree.

00:00:53.383 --> 00:00:55.510
But for people with paralysis
and disability,

00:00:55.552 --> 00:00:57.596
this technology can be life-changing.

00:01:00.932 --> 00:01:04.269
I’m very excited to introduce you
to Philip and Rodney.

00:01:04.894 --> 00:01:07.647
They both have a neurodegenerative
disease called ALS,

00:01:07.689 --> 00:01:10.942
means they can't move
their hands or speak clearly,

00:01:10.984 --> 00:01:12.569
but they can now text

00:01:12.611 --> 00:01:16.573
thanks to a brain-computer
interface or BCI.

00:01:17.324 --> 00:01:19.909
There were Philip’s brain signals
up on the screen.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:22.662
They’re connected
to their computers via Bluetooth.

00:01:22.704 --> 00:01:26.666
The device is fully internalized,
invisible to the outside world,

00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:29.169
and they learn to control the keyboard

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.130
with clicks directly coming
from their brain.

00:01:33.506 --> 00:01:37.177
Now BCIs conjure up images
of science fiction like "The Matrix"

00:01:37.177 --> 00:01:40.555
with a cable jacked up into your brain
through a hole in your skull.

00:01:40.597 --> 00:01:44.934
But I'm here to show you that the future
can be much more elegant than that.

00:01:46.019 --> 00:01:47.896
So we got this group chat going,

00:01:47.937 --> 00:01:49.522
which I thought was a great idea,

00:01:49.522 --> 00:01:51.983
until they started roasting me
about the TED Talk --

00:01:52.025 --> 00:01:53.026
(Laughter)

00:01:53.026 --> 00:01:55.111
Which they found hilarious.

00:01:55.153 --> 00:01:57.072
Thanks for the vote of confidence, guys,

00:01:57.072 --> 00:01:58.448
bloody Australians.

00:01:58.448 --> 00:01:59.616
(Laughter)

00:01:59.616 --> 00:02:02.744
Now you can see it's still quite slow
for them to type this way,

00:02:02.786 --> 00:02:06.998
but this is like dial up speeds
at the beginning of the Internet.

00:02:07.415 --> 00:02:09.209
This is a new Moore's Law.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.044
We're just getting started.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:15.965
(Laughter)

00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:18.134
(Applause)

00:02:18.134 --> 00:02:19.469
That's Philip.

00:02:21.346 --> 00:02:23.765
This has been the dream
of patients and caregivers,

00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:25.850
doctors and scientists, for decades,

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.477
and for good reason.

00:02:28.061 --> 00:02:31.022
You may know someone who's lost
the ability to use their hands,

00:02:31.064 --> 00:02:33.483
maybe from a stroke
or a spinal cord injury

00:02:33.525 --> 00:02:36.736
or multiple sclerosis, paralysis.

00:02:36.778 --> 00:02:38.446
It comes in all shapes and sizes,

00:02:38.446 --> 00:02:41.783
from minor inconvenience
to life-threatening.

00:02:43.576 --> 00:02:47.497
During my neurology residency,
I cared for a man in his 40s.

00:02:47.539 --> 00:02:50.917
He had a stroke and developed
locked-in syndrome.

00:02:51.793 --> 00:02:55.588
Meant he couldn't move his body,
except for his eyes, left or right.

00:02:57.716 --> 00:02:59.467
His brain still worked like yours.

00:02:59.509 --> 00:03:04.681
He could see and hear and think
and feel just like normal,

00:03:04.723 --> 00:03:08.101
but he couldn't move or speak ever again.

00:03:09.477 --> 00:03:12.564
And in what were horrific circumstances,

00:03:12.605 --> 00:03:15.400
we supported his wish
to be taken off life support.

00:03:16.943 --> 00:03:18.820
And so I've been wondering ever since,

00:03:18.862 --> 00:03:21.489
was there not anything else
that could have been done?

00:03:24.284 --> 00:03:27.328
Connection is a fundamental human need.

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:30.874
So many of our patients have lost
the ability to speak,

00:03:30.915 --> 00:03:33.543
let alone type, for years,

00:03:33.543 --> 00:03:36.421
and they so desperately want
to reconnect with their family,

00:03:36.421 --> 00:03:37.589
with their loved ones.

00:03:38.214 --> 00:03:40.592
You know what the main request we get is?

00:03:40.925 --> 00:03:42.510
Text messaging.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:43.803
And then email.

00:03:43.845 --> 00:03:46.055
Control over their smartphone.

00:03:46.097 --> 00:03:47.682
And shock horror,

00:03:47.724 --> 00:03:49.184
social media.

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:54.689
We've been speaking so much lately
about the flaws of these technologies,

00:03:54.731 --> 00:03:57.692
but for people with paralysis,
this is a return to life.

00:03:58.526 --> 00:04:00.779
BCIs make all of this possible.

00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:04.324
Now, part of the problem has been

00:04:04.365 --> 00:04:07.243
that BCIs typically require
invasive surgery.

00:04:07.577 --> 00:04:09.245
This is the Utah Array.

00:04:10.163 --> 00:04:12.457
This is designed similarly
to all other BCIs

00:04:12.457 --> 00:04:14.000
currently under development,

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.753
which require drilling needles
directly into the brain.

00:04:17.504 --> 00:04:21.090
Now, this has been the basis
of critical fundamental research

00:04:21.132 --> 00:04:22.801
over the last 20 years

00:04:22.842 --> 00:04:26.596
and the early proof that this
technology really can perform.

00:04:26.638 --> 00:04:29.974
But for patients,
it means open-brain surgery,

00:04:30.016 --> 00:04:32.519
which involves cutting through
the skull with a saw.

00:04:33.645 --> 00:04:37.023
And there are only about 150
functional neurosurgeons in the US

00:04:37.065 --> 00:04:39.067
that can perform this procedure.

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:41.820
Apart from the fact
that the recovery is tricky,

00:04:41.861 --> 00:04:44.697
the brain doesn't really like
having needles put into it.

00:04:45.406 --> 00:04:49.744
It develops this foreign-body tissue
rejection immune reaction over time.

00:04:50.245 --> 00:04:54.249
So I've been wondering,
is there any other way into the brain?

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:57.961
And there is, a secret back door.

00:04:59.212 --> 00:05:03.216
The blood vessels are the natural
highways into the brain.

00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:07.011
These are hollow tubes that connect
every corner of the brain.

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:12.225
The largest vein at the top there
is right next to the motor cortex.

00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:15.103
The exact part of the brain
that we want to connect to

00:05:15.144 --> 00:05:17.480
to restore control to the outside world.

00:05:17.856 --> 00:05:19.232
How cool is that?

00:05:21.442 --> 00:05:24.320
Now we already know how to travel
through the blood vessels.

00:05:24.362 --> 00:05:27.824
We've been doing it for 40 years,
mostly going to the heart.

00:05:28.658 --> 00:05:30.743
If anyone here today
has had a heart attack,

00:05:30.743 --> 00:05:33.454
there's a pretty good chance
you've had a stent.

00:05:34.497 --> 00:05:38.001
A stent is a metal scaffold
delivered through a catheter,

00:05:38.042 --> 00:05:40.920
which opens up like a flower
into the blood vessel.

00:05:41.754 --> 00:05:44.257
Millions of stents
are delivered each year,

00:05:44.299 --> 00:05:45.758
not in the OR,

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:49.512
but in the cath lab
or catheter laboratory.

00:05:50.847 --> 00:05:54.475
It's now common in the cath lab
to navigate up into the brain

00:05:54.517 --> 00:05:55.852
through the blood vessels.

00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:58.021
And there are 2,500 physicians

00:05:58.062 --> 00:06:00.523
who can now navigate their way
up into the brain.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.152
But what's really amazing about this

00:06:04.152 --> 00:06:07.196
is that for BCIs we already know

00:06:07.196 --> 00:06:10.241
that devices can be left
inside a blood vessel,

00:06:10.283 --> 00:06:12.952
cells grow over it,
incorporate it into the wall

00:06:12.994 --> 00:06:14.954
like a tattoo under the skin,

00:06:14.996 --> 00:06:17.373
and we're protected
from that immune reaction.

00:06:18.124 --> 00:06:21.544
This is part of the reason why our team
became the first in the world

00:06:21.544 --> 00:06:25.256
to receive a green light from the FDA
to conduct clinical trials

00:06:25.298 --> 00:06:27.717
of a permanently implanted BCI.

00:06:28.635 --> 00:06:33.431
(Applause)

00:06:33.932 --> 00:06:37.894
So what we had to do
was figure out a way to put a sensor,

00:06:37.936 --> 00:06:40.772
connected to this crosslinks of the stent

00:06:40.813 --> 00:06:43.107
that could record that brain activity.

00:06:43.107 --> 00:06:47.070
To do that, we had to do a complete
overhaul of stent manufacturing.

00:06:47.111 --> 00:06:48.279
This is the end result.

00:06:48.321 --> 00:06:49.739
I think it's very beautiful.

00:06:51.366 --> 00:06:53.701
Then connect it to a cable

00:06:53.743 --> 00:06:56.788
which brings the information
out of the brain

00:06:56.829 --> 00:07:00.083
and do it all in a way
that it can be delivered in the cath lab.

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:04.754
This way we can make BCI accessible
not to the thousands of people,

00:07:04.796 --> 00:07:07.632
but to the millions of people
who need this technology.

00:07:09.467 --> 00:07:13.429
So it wasn't easy, took us ten years,

00:07:13.471 --> 00:07:15.974
but I'm very excited to show you guys.

00:07:19.310 --> 00:07:21.437
We called this the Stentrode.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:33.950
(Applause)

00:07:34.784 --> 00:07:39.706
Graham Felstead, an incredible
human being suffering with ALS,

00:07:39.706 --> 00:07:41.457
became the first person in the world

00:07:41.457 --> 00:07:44.544
to receive and use one of these
brain-computer interfaces.

00:07:44.585 --> 00:07:47.422
And he has very generously
offered you, the world,

00:07:47.422 --> 00:07:50.717
a chance to see what it looks like
inside his brain.

00:07:50.717 --> 00:07:51.926
Would you like to see?

00:07:51.968 --> 00:07:53.094
Audience: Yes!

00:07:59.517 --> 00:08:01.436
TO: Seeing this video for the first time

00:08:01.436 --> 00:08:04.147
was one of the most incredible
moments of my life.

00:08:04.188 --> 00:08:06.024
I was standing in the cath lab,

00:08:06.065 --> 00:08:09.027
Dr. Peter Mitchell had just
completed the surgery

00:08:09.027 --> 00:08:11.404
and you can see the device,
the outline of device,

00:08:11.404 --> 00:08:13.573
sitting inside the blood vessel there.

00:08:13.614 --> 00:08:15.116
So this popped up on the screen

00:08:15.116 --> 00:08:19.871
and it just felt like we were witnessing
something new in the world.

00:08:20.621 --> 00:08:22.081
I had tingles down my spine,

00:08:22.123 --> 00:08:24.417
I've got them now thinking about it again.

00:08:24.459 --> 00:08:25.918
I turned to my colleague Pete,

00:08:25.918 --> 00:08:29.380
and I said something poetic
and profound like,

00:08:29.422 --> 00:08:31.424
"Pete, holy shit!"

00:08:31.466 --> 00:08:35.053
(Laughter)

00:08:35.053 --> 00:08:38.056
And then two hours later,
something even more amazing happened.

00:08:38.097 --> 00:08:41.642
Graham woke up,
and he asked, “Am I alive?”

00:08:42.727 --> 00:08:45.229
And our nurse Kristine
broke out in tears of relief.

00:08:45.271 --> 00:08:47.106
It was a phenomenal moment.

00:08:48.691 --> 00:08:50.151
Once it's in place,

00:08:50.193 --> 00:08:54.405
it's connected to this tiny antenna
that sits under the skin in the chest.

00:08:55.114 --> 00:08:57.033
This collects the raw brain data

00:08:57.033 --> 00:08:59.577
and sends it out of the body wirelessly

00:08:59.619 --> 00:09:02.163
to then connect with external devices.

00:09:03.039 --> 00:09:04.957
It's always on and ready to go.

00:09:04.999 --> 00:09:07.293
Kind of like how your brain
is meant to work.

00:09:09.087 --> 00:09:11.005
So here's how it works.

00:09:11.923 --> 00:09:16.803
Our engineers work with our patients
to decode specific movements.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.764
So we tell the patient,
“Press down your foot.”

00:09:19.806 --> 00:09:22.058
So they'll repeatedly
press down their foot.

00:09:22.058 --> 00:09:26.020
You won't see the foot moving
because they're paralyzed,

00:09:26.062 --> 00:09:27.605
but we've been able to determine

00:09:27.605 --> 00:09:31.484
which brain signals are generally linked
to “Press down your foot.”

00:09:32.318 --> 00:09:35.279
The black dotted line is the moment
of pressing down the foot,

00:09:35.321 --> 00:09:39.075
and you can see the brain signal
is different before to after,

00:09:39.117 --> 00:09:42.537
which means we can turn
that into a switch.

00:09:43.621 --> 00:09:46.499
Now we repeat this for several
different types of movements,

00:09:46.499 --> 00:09:49.377
say, open/close your hand
or pincer-grip your finger.

00:09:49.418 --> 00:09:51.754
Now, that may not seem like much,

00:09:51.796 --> 00:09:55.424
but these become the building blocks
for every single interaction

00:09:55.424 --> 00:09:58.594
on a digital device
that is needed for control.

00:10:01.305 --> 00:10:03.224
Converted to

00:10:03.266 --> 00:10:07.311
click, up, down, left, right,
menu, back, etc.

00:10:08.146 --> 00:10:11.399
But what's really amazing
is that to some degree,

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:12.859
this process,

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:15.069
our brain signals, are universal.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:18.364
So the brain signal
for “Press down your foot” for me

00:10:18.364 --> 00:10:20.533
is the same as it is for you.

00:10:21.617 --> 00:10:24.453
Now this means that we're creating
a dictionary of the brain

00:10:24.453 --> 00:10:26.122
across all humans.

00:10:26.831 --> 00:10:29.333
This is going to make BCI truly scalable.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.213
As Philip once said to me,

00:10:34.255 --> 00:10:36.549
"It's kind of like learning
how to ride a bike.

00:10:36.591 --> 00:10:39.218
It takes a bit of practice,
but once you're rolling,

00:10:39.218 --> 00:10:40.887
it becomes natural.

00:10:40.928 --> 00:10:44.765
Now I just look on the screen
where I want to click and I'm texting,

00:10:44.807 --> 00:10:46.517
messaging the world via Twitter."

00:10:46.517 --> 00:10:48.436
But Graham,

00:10:48.477 --> 00:10:51.147
he said, as his ALS was progressing,

00:10:51.189 --> 00:10:55.818
that it gave him immense comfort
to know that even if his body was failing,

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.113
he was always going to be able
to tell his wife that he loved her.

00:11:02.533 --> 00:11:03.993
In the future,

00:11:03.993 --> 00:11:07.788
I'm really excited about the breakthroughs
BCI could deliver to other conditions

00:11:07.788 --> 00:11:10.833
like epilepsy, depression and dementia.

00:11:10.875 --> 00:11:12.668
But beyond that,

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:15.004
what is this going to mean for humanity?

00:11:15.922 --> 00:11:20.218
What's really got me thinking
is the future of communication.

00:11:22.178 --> 00:11:23.512
Take emotion.

00:11:25.014 --> 00:11:29.101
Have you ever considered
how hard it is to express how you feel?

00:11:29.936 --> 00:11:32.063
You have to self-reflect,

00:11:32.104 --> 00:11:34.398
package the emotion into words

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.276
and then use the muscles of your mouth
to speak those words.

00:11:37.276 --> 00:11:39.987
But you really just want
someone to know how you feel.

00:11:40.988 --> 00:11:42.907
For some people with certain conditions,

00:11:42.949 --> 00:11:44.325
that's impossible.

00:11:44.367 --> 00:11:46.869
So what if, rather than using your words,

00:11:46.911 --> 00:11:51.707
you could throw your emotion
just for a few seconds?

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:54.627
And have them really feel how you feel.

00:11:56.796 --> 00:11:58.756
At that moment, we would have realized

00:11:58.798 --> 00:12:02.718
that the necessary use of words
to express our current state of being

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.720
was always going to fall short.

00:12:06.722 --> 00:12:09.308
The full potential of the brain
would then be unlocked.

00:12:11.644 --> 00:12:13.771
But for right now,

00:12:13.771 --> 00:12:18.943
BCI is about restoring the lives
of millions of people with paralysis.

00:12:18.943 --> 00:12:21.654
After years of feeling trapped,

00:12:21.696 --> 00:12:26.492
this technology promises the return
of autonomy and independence.

00:12:27.285 --> 00:12:30.121
But what I really mean is dignity.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:32.248
Thank you.

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:38.921
(Applause)

